  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a progression of two-dimensional material systems, arranged from simpler to more complex structures. At the left is a single flat layer, representing an isolated 2D sheet. Moving right, the image shows stacked van der Waals heterostructures, where several thin layers are combined while remaining distinct. The next stage adds more chemical and structural modification: one example shows a layer decorated with attached molecular groups above and below, and another shows a surface covered with many small particles, indicating functionalized layers and hybrid heterostructures.\n\nAt the far right, the materials become irregular mixed assemblies, labeled as hybrid materials and composites. These appear as tangled combinations of differently shaped components, suggesting a move from ordered layered systems to more complex nanocomposites.\n\nTwo large arrows frame the whole diagram. The top arrow indicates increasing complexity from left to right. Along the bottom, the figure links this materials evolution to application areas, shifting from “2D physics” for simple single layers, through “2D electronics” for layered and functionalized structures, toward “2D nanocomposites” for the most complex hybrids. Overall, the image summarizes how adding stacking, surface chemistry, and mixing expands both structural complexity and functionality.",
